# Internationalization (i18n)

This project uses `@nuxtjs/i18n` with Dutch (NL) as default and English (EN) as secondary language. No URL prefix strategy.

## File Structure

```
i18n/locales/
  nl.ts    # Dutch translations (default locale)
  en.ts    # English translations
```

## Translation Keys

- Use **camelCase** for keys: `signInDescription`, `userCreatedSuccessfully`
- Group by feature: `auth.loginTitle`, `settings.users.deleteConfirm`
- Keep NL and EN files in sync: every key must exist in both

## Usage in Templates

```vue
<template>
    <!-- In templates -->
    <h1>{{ $t('auth.loginTitle') }}</h1>
    <UButton :label="$t('common.save')" />
</template>

<script setup>
const { t } = useI18n()

// In script
const message = t('auth.loginSuccess')
</script>
```

## Zod Schemas with i18n

Use `computed` schemas so error messages are reactive to locale changes:

```ts
const { t } = useI18n()

const schema = computed(() => z.object({
    email: z.email(t('validation.invalidEmail')),
    name: z.string().min(1, t('validation.required')),
}))
```

## Rules

- **ALL user-facing text must use i18n**; never hardcode strings in templates
- Date formatting: use `Intl.DateTimeFormat` with the current locale
- Number formatting: use `Intl.NumberFormat` with the current locale
- Zod validation messages must use `t()` inside `computed` schemas
- Button labels, tooltips, placeholders, error messages: all i18n
